<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmed Urabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Orabi, Urabi Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmed Urabi was an Egyptian army officer who led a nationalist movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive</a> Tewfik and European control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s finances. Of peasant origin, he opposed the dominance of Turco-Circassian officers and became minister of war in 1882. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> invaded, defeated his army at Tel el-Kebir in September 1882 and occupied Egypt, and Urabi was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> until 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-urabi.jpg" alt="Ahmed Urabi" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabi_(1906)_-_TIMEA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egypt’s debts led to European financial control and, after the revolt led by <b>Ahmed Urabi</b>, to British occupation in 1882, while France made Tunisia a protectorate by the Treaty of Bardo in 1881.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ahmed Urabi's</b> nationalist revolt led Britain to invade in 1882, and Lord Cromer effectively governed Egypt from 1883 to 1907.</blockquote><a href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zagazig, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.59,31.5&amp;year=1886" aria-label="Zagazig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5366 1048 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmed Urabi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5816 1318h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5816 1318h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.59,31.5&amp;year=1886">What was here in 1886? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are not slaves, and we shall never more be inherited from this day forth…&#8221;</p><footer>His reply to the Khedive Tewfik at the Abdin Palace, 9 September 1881 &#183; English translation in W. S. Blunt, Secret History of the English Occupation of Egypt, 1907 (translator not named) &#183; his own account of the day, given to Blunt &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/41373/pg41373-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
